ription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction-changed"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 2.1, "y": 67.5, "width": 39.6, "height": 29.1, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 2.1, "y": 67.5, "width": 39.6, "height": 29.1, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 2.1, "y": 67.5, "width": 39.6, "height": 29.1, "rotation": 0, "text": ["Lenard's data indicated that there was some threshold frequency, below which no electrons were ejected. Above that frequency, the electrons' energy rose linearly with the frequency of incident light, but was independent of the intensity of the light."]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 39.5, "y": 11.6, "width": 57.8, "height": 38.6, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 39.5, "y": 11.6, "width": 57.8, "height": 38.6, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 39.5, "y": 11.6, "width": 57.8, "height": 38.6, "rotation": 0, "text": ["In his experiments, Lenard applied a tunable voltage V across conducting plates. When one of the plates was irradiated with ultraviolet light (UV), electrons were ejected and traveled to the other plate, completing a circuit (hence current was measured in the ammeter A). By adjusting the voltage, Lenard could measure how much energy the electrons had upon being ejected from the plate (\"stopping voltage\"), and compare that with the frequency of the radiation that had shone onto the plate."]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 40.4, "y": 56.6, "width": 57.2, "height": 41.7, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_5", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 40.4, "y": 56.6, "width": 57.2, "height": 41.7, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["image"]}, "id": "BB_5", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 40.4, "y": 56.6, "width": 57.2, "height": 41.7, "rotation": 0, "text": [""]}, "id": "BB_5", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 41.09907120743034, "y": 58.20433436532506, "width": 7.314241486068091, "height": 13.622291021671815, "rotation": 0}, "id": "ZI0POnyJUn", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 41.09907120743034, "y": 58.20433436532506, "width": 7.314241486068091, "height": 13.622291021671815, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "ZI0POnyJUn", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 41.09907120743034, "y": 58.20433436532506, "width": 7.314241486068091, "height": 13.622291021671815, "ro